"[2610.22380] Trust but Verify: Designing Region-Inhomogeneous Forward Dynamics for Discrete Diffusion (https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22380)\nciteturn41247view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22380\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 170\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Machine Learning\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 2 Oct 2026]\nL9: # Title:Trust but Verify: Designing Region-Inhomogeneous Forward Dynamics for Discrete Diffusion\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Gunmay Jhingran L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Trust but Verify: Designing Region-Inhomogeneous Forward Dynamics for Discrete Diffusion, by Gunmay Jhingran\nL14: \nL15: cite6†View PDF cite7†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Continuous diffusion treats the forward process as a design space; discrete language diffusion often uses absorbing corruption, leaving context fully trusted throughout generation. This makes trust binary: corrupted context is copied as ground truth, propagating errors into generated spans. We reopen this design space by combining absorbing and uniform-mixing kernels with region-dependent rates.\nL17: Missing positions follow absorbing dynamics, while context undergoes low-rate random substitution, exposing the model to observed but uncertain tokens during training. At inference, a heuristic sampler re-injects noise into suspicious context under an annealed intensity and freezes context updates late in the trajectory. An 8,000-step finetune of a pretrained 170M-parameter masked diffusion model takes under five hours on a 6 GB laptop GPU.\nL18: The base model assigns roughly 50% probability to observed tokens even when they are uniform-random garbage. For uniform-random corruption, finetuning detects corrupted context with 0.96 recall and 0.91 precision, improves infill token-F1 by 3.8 points at 10% corruption and 6.4 points at 20%, and removes the accuracy dip next to corruptions. Clean conditioning shows no measurable regression, with a false-edit rate below 0.4%.\nL19: Gains for plausible corruptions are weaker: about 0.5 F1 points at 10% corruption, without statistical significance. Our results suggest that detectability and damage depend jointly on departure from the data conditional: nearby noise is hard to detect and semantically mild, while distant noise is conspicuous and destructive. We interpret absorbing diffusion as the infinite-contrast limit of a trust axis, analogous to the signal-to-noise gap between known and unknown regions in diffusion inpainting.\nL20: In our setting of a 170M model and a single text domain, intermediate trust is reached by changing forward dynamics rather than architecture.\nL21: Comments:  | Accepted to 40th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2026). Workshop: AI for Stochastic Dynamics. 10 pages\nL22: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL23: Cite as:  | cite8†arXiv:2610.22380 [cs.LG]\nL24:    | (or cite9†arXiv:2610.22380v1 [cs.LG] for this version)\nL25:    | cite10†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2610.22380†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL26: ## Submission history\nL27: \nL28: From: Gunmay Jhingran Mr [cite11†view email ]\nL29: [v1] Fri, 2 Oct 2026 15:14:44 UTC (216 KB)\nL30: \nL31: Full-text links:\nL32: \nL33: ## Access Paper:\nL34: \nL35: View a PDF of the paper titled Trust but Verify: Designing Region-Inhomogeneous Forward Dynamics for Discrete Diffusion, by Gunmay Jhingran\nL36: \nL37:   * cite6†View PDF L38:   * cite7†HTML (experimental) L39:   * cite12†TeX Source L40: \nL41: cite13†view license†creativecommons.org L42: \nL43: ### Additional Features\nL44: \nL45:   * cite14†Audio Summary L46: ### Current browse context: