rg · cite50†Accessibility†info.arxiv.org · cite51†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org L168: \nL169: Major funding support from\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2610.22438] Rank Measures of Fine-Tuning Updates Need a Noise Baseline (https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22438)\nciteturn41247view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22438\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 169\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Machine Learning\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 15 Oct 2026]\nL9: # Title:Rank Measures of Fine-Tuning Updates Need a Noise Baseline\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Muhammad Usman Altaf L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Rank Measures of Fine-Tuning Updates Need a Noise Baseline, by Muhammad Usman Altaf\nL14: \nL15: cite6†View PDF cite7†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Studies of what fine-tuning does to a language model often summarise the weight update $\\Delta W$ by its rank. We compare the rank measures used for this against the same measures on a random matrix of the same shape, and against two harder references: the pretrained weights, and matched fine-tunes without alignment content. Measures that count directions put our fine-tuning updates only a little below noise; stable rank, computed from the same singular values, puts them far below.\nL17: Both are right: an update has a few strong directions on top of a long tail. The pattern holds for thirteen released pairs up to 8B parameters, and it is not a mark of alignment: with training held fixed, instruction data does not stand out from Wikipedia or code, DPO gives the same update whether its preference labels are real or half swapped at random (one model, modest preference strength), and scrambled text gives the most concentrated update of all.\nL18: Applied to a recent claim that post-training is superficial because the effective rank of the weights barely changes, the baselines show that a random update of the same size leaves that rank almost unchanged while wrecking the model, and that an edit built on the effective rank of the update has no measurable effect in our tests. Rank measures of weight updates need a noise baseline, and claims about alignment need a control without alignment content.\nL19: Comments:  | 24 pages, 6 figures, 15 tables. Under review at TMLR\nL20: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL21: ACM (Association of Computing Machinery Classification) classes:  | I.2.6; I.2.7\nL22: Cite as:  | cite8†arXiv:2610.22438 [cs.LG]\nL23:    | (or cite9†arXiv:2610.22438v1 [cs.LG] for this version)\nL24:    | cite10†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2610.22438†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Muhammad Usman Altaf [cite11†view email ]\nL28: [v1] Thu, 15 Oct 2026 13:52:20 UTC (170 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled Rank Measures of Fine-Tuning Updates Need a Noise Baseline, by Muhammad Usman Altaf\nL35: \nL36:   * cite6†View PDF L37:   * cite7†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite12†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite13†view license†creativecommons.org L41: \nL42: ### Additional Features\nL43: \nL44:   * cite14†Audio Summary L45: ### Current browse context:\nL46: \nL47: cs.LG\nL48: \nL49: cite15†< prev |   cite16†next > L50: \nL51: cite17†new | cite18†recent | cite19†2026-10 L52: \nL53: Change to browse by:\nL54: \nL55: cite20†cs L56: \nL57: ### References & Citations\nL58: \nL59:   * cite21†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L60:   * cite22†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L61:   * cite23†Semantic Scholar†api.sem