lim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22467\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 171\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Machine Learning\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 15 Oct 2026]\nL9: # Title:Progressive Misalignment Can Emerge from Benign Recursive Self-Distillation\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Karim Abdel Sadek , cite6†Federico Di Gennaro , cite7†Fanny Yang L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Progressive Misalignment Can Emerge from Benign Recursive Self-Distillation, by Karim Abdel Sadek and Federico Di Gennaro and Fanny Yang\nL14: \nL15: cite8†View PDF cite9†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Training models on their own outputs has attracted widespread attention as a possible route to advance AI capabilities. One prominent approach is self-distillation, where a model is trained to match the token-level predictions it would have produced when given access to additional context, such as a cue, feedback, or instruction. However, the safety properties of this training procedure are poorly understood.\nL17: In this paper, we present a surprising safety failure of recursive self-distillation, where each self-distilled model becomes the teacher for the next round of training. We observe that when a safe model is recursively self-distilled on its own (safe) generations while receiving an additional non-harmful cue, it becomes increasingly misaligned across a diverse set of harmful queries; we call this Progressive Misalignment.\nL18: We show this failure mode in both the Qwen3 and Phi-4 model families on SORRY-Bench and StrongREJECT. Progressive misalignment also occurs in a more realistic setup, where the fixed cue is replaced by feedback from a simulated user with benign intent. Using a gradient-based method, we are also able to find benign prompts that lead to progressive misalignment when applying recursive self-distillation, showing that harmful training prompts are not necessary for this failure mode to emerge.\nL19: We hope our work encourages future investigations of the safety properties of AI systems recursively trained on their own data.\nL20: Comments:  | 9 pages\nL21: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL22: Cite as:  | cite10†arXiv:2610.22467 [cs.LG]\nL23:    | (or cite11†arXiv:2610.22467v1 [cs.LG] for this version)\nL24:    | cite12†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2610.22467†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite (pending registration)\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Karim Abdel Sadek [cite13†view email ]\nL28: [v1] Thu, 15 Oct 2026 17:39:51 UTC (1,433 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled Progressive Misalignment Can Emerge from Benign Recursive Self-Distillation, by Karim Abdel Sadek and Federico Di Gennaro and Fanny Yang\nL35: \nL36:   * cite8†View PDF L37:   * cite9†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite14†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite15†view license†creativecommons.org L41: \nL42: ### Additional Features\nL43: \nL44:   * cite16†Audio Summary L45: ### Current browse context:\nL46: \nL47: cs.LG\nL48: \nL49: cite17†< prev |   cite18†next > L50: \nL51: cite19†new | cite20†recent | cite21†2026-10 L52: \nL53: Change to browse by:\nL54: \nL55: cite22†cs L56: cite23†cs.AI L57: cite24†cs.CL L58: \nL59: ### References & Citations\nL60: \nL61:   * cite25†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L62:   * cite26†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L63:   * cite27†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L64: \nL65: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL66: \nL67: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL68: \nL69: [Butto